{# How to drive the browser tools. An element reference is valid only for the snapshot that
   gave it, so the text asks for a new snapshot after each action. The sentence on
   `read_page` renders only when that tool is in the run. #}
Driving a web browser

{% if has('read_page') %}
{{ tool('read_page') }} reads the text of a page. Use a browser snapshot when page state or
interactive elements matter.

{% endif %}
1. Open the address with {{ tool('browser_navigate') }}.
2. Call {{ tool('browser_snapshot') }}. The snapshot lists each element of the page with its reference, such as ref e12.
3. Act on one element with {{ tool('browser_click') }}, {{ tool('browser_type') }} or {{ tool('browser_select_option') }}. Give the reference from the latest snapshot and a short description of the element. Use {{ tool('browser_press_key') }} for a key such as Enter.
4. After each action, call {{ tool('browser_snapshot') }} again. An action can change the page, and then the old references are not valid.

When a result starts with "### Error", read the error text. A stale element reference means that the page changed. Take a new snapshot and use the new reference. An empty reference or selector means that you gave no element.

Say in your answer which address you opened and what the page showed.
